    "turn_text": "and there's a curtain that separates each bed. And in one of the beds was a young girl. She was about one to two years old. And my wife was getting really anxious because she didn't see the young girl's parents. And she kept asking the nurses, like, where are her parents? Why aren't they coming and seeing her? Because we were there for a couple of days, and we didn't really see the parents that much. And the nurses finally said, you know, we can't tell you much about another patient. Just know they love her, but they're working. Like, they can't afford to miss work. We learned that that young girl had meningitis of the brain. I mean, a very serious condition. In our country, healthcare costs are so expensive and not a lot of employers are able to offer, you know, like an extended paid leave while your kid is sick. So we've seen up close and personal, you know, the best and too often the worst of our healthcare system. And it's a responsibility to want to do something about it. All right, well, I wasn't going to jump in this fast with policy. But since we're out of health care, let's talk about health care. I know that you support a universal option, not necessarily single payer. Talk about that. And what has this experience having these kids and seeing this informed in terms of what you think? the public wants. Yeah. And so I've thought a lot about this. You know, and I've heard pretty good arguments about, you know, a single payer system and what that could do and make sure every single person has health care and, you",
