    "turn_idx": 72,
    "turn_text": "and you have to tell them. They wouldn't move. And at the very next council meeting, the mayor told my dad, and this is a public open meeting, You have to reverse those tickets, or you're going to be fired. And so he refused, has played out for a couple months, and he was fired. And we picked up our little family, and we moved out West. And honestly, I always thought my dad had told the story and the light most favorable to him. We all often do, you know, in our own stories. But when I went back to Iowa in 2016 for the 2016 election working on campaigns there, I looked up the Des Moines Register archives and the Algona Times archives. And they really covered the story is he was fighting the case. It was covered in the state. It was covered in the state. And there are Des Moines Register reporters who remember it, who are still there. And they covered his, like he was, you know, he was trying to clean up this town and it was corrupt and he paid the price. And it taught me that whether you're the chief of police of, whether you're the, it taught me whether you're the mayor of Algona, Iowa or the president of the United States. no one's about the law. And that was a good lesson to learn as a five-year-old, not knowing then that I would later go on the Judiciary",
