    "turn_idx": 93,
    "turn_text": "Recognizing I wouldn't be elected, I wouldn't have a vote, but I would always be asked how I would vote. And I figured that seat at the table would at least allow me to be a voice for the students and build a partnership with the council. And we were able to get that done. And I served as the first student liaison on the council. And the first initiative I worked on was community college, was community cleanup days. So Sunday mornings after all the parties, I got the fraternities and sororities who needed civic credits to fulfill their requirements. Okay, I'm gonna ask if you had any friends left or this. Yes, exactly. So we would all just be dragging out there at 7 a.m., 8 a.m., cleaning up the mess that everyone had made the night before. But it showed, it engendered goodwill with the community because they saw the students carrying about the streets that they had dirty the night before. And it was just something small like that, but it got me involved locally. Did you at that age kind of dealing with folks, I assume, who are far older than you on the council?",
