    "turn_text": "cause of death. For a white child and so I think it's it's investing immediately in those communities I so I was in the south side of Chicago a couple weeks ago in this neighborhood called Englewood and I met with a woman named Tamara Manasa and she has taken over a couple blocks and told me that the kids don't even go to school because they don't feel safe on the journey to school because it's so dangerous. So she's found some of these condemned corner lots. She's found some of these corner lots and had them condemned and has brought storage containers to them and has set up these pop-up kitchens and pop-up classrooms essentially to create a sense of community. And she told me that she recently had a second chance prom. And she said a lot of these kids got expelled from school or kicked out of school and she wanted them to have a prom so they got to know each other and respect each other. And she said that a lot of them told her that they'd never worn a suit before in their life and they only expected to wear one when they were in the box. That's what they told her. And to me that represents the hopelessness in so many of America's cities. And I support background checks for firearms. I support an assault weapons ban. But that's not going to solve the structural issue of hopelessness and a lack of investment And I think because I went to law school, you know in Baltimore because I've worked as a prosecutor in Oakland These communities are not invisible to me I want to stay with gun control for a second, because that is something that you've really made a centerpiece of your campaign. And I think it's unique to your campaign. Talk about this buyback program. Like, what's the idea here? What would it cost? And how would it work? Yeah. It's not my idea to ban and buyback assault weapons. The Australians are decades ahead of us on this. They had a mass shooting in the 90s, and immediately",
