    "turn_text": "of our neighbors with one condition. You had to have voted in the last election, and it had to be also the first political event you've ever been to. So it was Republicans, Democrats, independents in a state where kids get the day off when hunting season opens. So they value their right to hunt. And so I made the case for a ban and a buyback, and I was ready to brace for impact. And it was just crickets. And finally, one guy raised his hand and he said, yeah, I get what you're saying. I don't really have a problem with it. But he said exactly what you said. He said, I just think this problem has gotten too big for us to solve. And when he said that, I said, I can work with that. Because I'm in this to solve the unsolvable. If you think government can't do it, I want to find a way to do it, you're not telling me it is my God-given right to have a weapon that shoots dozens of rounds in a minute that would never be used for hunting, only used to mow down people. I need to have that gun. He wasn't saying that at all. He just said the problem's gotten too big to solve. So it was hopelessness. I can work with that. What I found is that the people who are against this are a very vocal, tweeting, bowling minority. And that the NRA has led us to believe that addressing this issue is a divisive tactic. That addressing this issue is a divisive hot stove that you don't touch. And that's a tactic. Because if we think that you're going to pay a political price for doing something about this, then we're not going to touch it. And they win. But I'll tell you, Marisa, I've gone, as I said, to 26 states in the last two years. I've been on the road since I announced this run for president all over the country. People come up to me about every imaginable issue. All of the haters that I see and read on Twitter and Facebook and Instagram who have like their holding a salt rifle as their profile picture, like we shouldn't be concerned about",
