    "turn_idx": 177,
    "turn_text": "issues, I have found that the dirty maps and the dirty money keep us from taking them on. And I'll give you an example. When I was a freshman, I passed, I had more bills signing a law than any other Democrat, and I was in the minority, so I worked a lot with Republicans, including one that helped our Filipino community here as Typhoon Haiyan wiped out the Philippines, and it was a tax incentive bill for people to, you know, give, you know, charity dollars to foundations that would help over there, and it just incentivized that to help an allied nation. So, you know, I worked on a tax bill. I worked on something as easy as naming a post office after a local veteran. On the bigger stuff is where you see the money pour in. San Francisco Chronicle about five years ago had this six part series on rare earth elements. We're starting to hear about them again. Rare earth elements are used in so many of the devices we rely on, from laptops to cell phones, jet engines, anti-missile systems. And the Chinese, at the time and still today, controlled about 95 plus percent of the exports. And they were continuing to choke off the exports to raise the cost or force a US company to relocate over to China. The Chronicle found mines in the United States that we could safely use to get these rare earth elements. So it would bring down the costs for US manufacturers and not have to rely on China. I wrote legislation that took about six months working with Republicans to create a program at the Department of Justice and even a loan guarantee program so businesses would go into the mines. The Republican said, we like this program, credit to Kevin McCarthy, who gave me a vote on it. He said, I'll take, if you take out the loan guarantee part, because Cylindra, right, like dirty word for Republicans, loan guarantee, even though the program made more money than it lost. They didn't want anything with loan guarantees. We'll still create your program. We'll get the U.S. invested in rare earth elements. I was excited. It was a big issue. We were really having our lunch eaten by China on it. The day it came up for a vote. Again, six months' worth of work with the science committee chairman, Kevin McCarthy, the majority leader for the Republicans. The day of the vote The Heritage Foundation and Club for Growth send out an alert to their members and they tell them, call your member of Congress, tell them to vote against this new loan guarantee government program, loan guarantees wasn't a part of it, tell them to vote against it or they will be scored negatively. Scored negatively is like a Washington speak for, we will give them a bad letter grade and If their letter grade is so low, we will fund a more conservative candidate to run against them. So Kevin McCarthy said to me, he said, your bill is going to lose now. I suggest you just pull it. And I told him, I said, I think I can save this. And he was looking at a freshman lawmaker, and he was probably like, I really am like, sure, go ahead, try and save this. He knew the influence of the money. And I wanted to talk to my Republican friends, eyeball to eyeball. And so the vote is going on. I'm scrambling on the floor, telling them we can't lose to China. We want to get in the game in this. And the number of my colleagues who looked me in the eye and said, I like what you're trying to do. I'm just afraid of how I'm going to get scored. It was a punch of the gut. And I thought, if you're just going to turn over your vote to the club for growth, just give me your voting card. That's what you're doing. But I saw the power of money in our system, and that on these big issues, until we strip down to the studs, the Citizens United ruling, until we get rid of, we have to do that next. And until we have independent redistricting in every single state, not just California, we're not going to get out of this front. Well, somebody in the audience asked, how would you break the log jam that Mitch McConnell has created in the Senate? I think it's an excellent question, because I think that we blame a lot in the democratic stronghold that is California on Trump. But if you look at the way that McConnell has really held his caucus together has held up the Merrick Garland nomination, has really just refused to bring up any legislation around gun control, for example, that",
